WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:07.420
The verdict is in and it looks as though Hollywood's
ill-fated quest to adapt an anime into a successful

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:10.150
live-action feature is set to stumble on.

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:16.119
Netflix was always taking a chance by remaking
a property held in such high regard by anime

00:00:16.119 --> 00:00:21.920
fans both at home in Japan and around the
world, and, sadly, their English language,

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:27.500
U.S.-based take on the anime classic Death
Note has completely failed to take off.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:28.540
Here’s why.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:30.020
Trying too hard

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:36.280
The smash success of Stranger Things on Netflix
recently led to a burst of '80s nostalgia

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:42.600
in film and television, and Death Note shamelessly
hitches a ride on that particular bandwagon.

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:48.700
The film even employs the classic, almost
cliched slow-mo opening scene, reminiscent

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:53.610
of the famous "Head Over Heels" high school
scene from indie classic Donnie Darko.

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:59.460
Berlin's "Take My Breath Away" and Air Supply's
version of "The Power of Love" both show up,

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:04.580
of course, albeit only in what amounts to
little more than a shallow attempt to cultivate

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:07.780
style in the absence of any real substance.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:13.560
A horror director by trade, Death Note’s
director Adam Wingard dabbled in '80s art-house

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:19.859
aesthetics for his 2014 thriller hybrid The
Guest, but Death Note borrows just a little

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:25.200
too heavily from the dark neon hues of films
like Drive and Cold In July.

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:26.680
The scream

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:34.500
the casting of former Nickelodeon star Nat
Wolff as leading man Light Turner was an uninspired

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:35.000
one.

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:39.800
The actor's performance has been one of the
main sticking points for fans of the original

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:44.200
anime, with one scene in particular often
singled out for ridicule.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:49.680
When death god Ryuk first reveals himself
to Light, he does so with an impressive display

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:50.560
of his powers.

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:56.560
Wolff's reaction is well, let’s just say
it’s attracted some understandable criticism.

00:01:57.740 --> 00:01:58.380
"Ahh."

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:05.880
Not exactly in keeping with the manga’s
ice-cool version of the character.

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:07.740
White light

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:12.280
But that wasn’t the film’s only problem
with Death Note’s protagonist.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:17.120
Light is such an interesting character to
follow in the manga and anime because he isn't

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:21.989
just some kid who’s come across a book that
can kill people he's also an immensely clever

00:02:21.989 --> 00:02:27.659
sociopath who relishes the chance to use the
Death Note, and keeps its existence completely

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:29.660
secret for that very reason.

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:35.220
Netflix’s Light is neither a genius nor
a sociopath, and only ends up becoming Kira

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:40.019
because he's pushed down the path of darkness
by other characters with agendas of their

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:43.379
own — not because of the darker aspects
of his own character.

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:45.640
“Is that what we’re going to do with the
note light?

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:46.820
Rules and warnings?”

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:49.180
Ryuk’s purpose

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:54.320
To keep Light as innocent as possible in all
this, the filmmakers needed to shift the blame

00:02:54.329 --> 00:02:59.700
for the plot’s death and destruction onto
someone else and Ryuk must have been a natural

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:00.860
choice of scapegoat.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:06.020
Light's girlfriend Mia certainly plays her
part in convincing him to go crazy with the

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:11.129
the Note, but the death god is painted as
the true instigator of the chaos which takes

00:03:11.129 --> 00:03:12.900
place in the Netflix remake.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:17.700
He's the one who convinces Light to commit
his very first murder, and he continues to

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:19.600
manipulate him from there on in.

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:24.939
In the original Death Note, however, Ryuk’s
role is not to pressure Light into using the

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:30.980
Note, but to simply tag along mostly because
he’s bored and finds Light's behavior interesting.

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:32.800
A quick note

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:38.500
At 100 minutes, Wingard's film wasn't much
longer than your typical horror or comedy

00:03:38.510 --> 00:03:44.159
flick and constituted far too short a timespan
to do the detailed source material any kind

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:44.880
of justice.

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:50.129
These constraints meant that certain vital
plot points had to be abandoned and relationships

00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:54.260
fast-tracked, the result being a movie that
felt completely rushed.

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:56.300
Death Note is not light reading.

00:03:56.540 --> 00:04:01.300
After all, there’s a reason the Japanese
live-action version was split into two separate

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:05.220
films and even they had to skip certain elements
of the manga.

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:10.280
In the end, it might have served the Netflix
version better to have been produced as an

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:13.980
Original Series, played out over ten, hour-long
episodes.

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:19.259
This problem with the remake was best summed-up
in a review by the L.A. Times shortly after

00:04:19.259 --> 00:04:20.259
Death Note’s debut.

00:04:20.259 --> 00:04:25.320
It said: "For a film 10 years in the making,
it sure feels like everyone involved is in

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:26.720
a hurry to get it over with."

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:28.840
Forcing love

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:34.180
Another character who barely resembles the
version on which they were based is Mia, who

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:39.120
is turned from an innocent bystander into
a genuine monster for the remake.

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:44.940
Wingard's Death Note paints her as totally
unhinged and entirely willing to manipulate

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:49.260
Light into using the Death Note without a
second thought for the repercussions.

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:54.860
Netflix’s Death Note also turned Mia and
Light into some kind of tragic love story,

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:58.500
when in the source material their relationship
was anything but.

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:01.100
“I love you so much.”

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:03.320
“You do?”

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:09.500
Again, this seems like an attempt to shoehorn
Hollywood storytelling conventions into a

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:13.360
story which only succeeded by getting rid
of them in the first place.

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:18.750
In the real Death Note, the only thing Light
ever falls in love with is power.

